{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's central mechanistic claim that a shared checklist induces PERFECTLY correlated errors across non-experts is empirically false. Training and applying the same rules does not guarantee identical interpretations or execution. Within a single evaluation, panels of trained non-experts frequently show measurable inter-rater disagreement in scoring, boundary coding, or ambiguous item interpretation. If panelists apply the checklist with even stochastic variability, the effective n > 1, so aggregation can still reduce random noise independent of checklist bias. SRC_001 concedes this possibility in its counterconditions but its main claim and mechanism treat the correlation as perfect, which overstates the case and fails to account for the reliability gain the problem asks it to address.","counterexample":[{"aggregation":"majority vote yields correct answer; effective n > 1","panelist_A":"scores item as 'present' under checklist rule 3","panelist_B":"scores same item as 'absent' under same rule 3 due to ambiguous wording"}],"target":"7ac9dbdb3b18573fe3910019332ce6f3255bfaf16f769b002d904d54a73c7626"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's proposed within-evaluation test is self-undermining for the panel case. It claims genuine reliability is distinguished from mere compliance by perturbing decision-relevant features and observing whether the panel's judgment changes. But trained non-experts following an explicit checklist are constrained to the features the checklist encodes. If the perturbation is to features the checklist does not include, the panel CANNOT respond, even if the perturbation is decision-relevant to the true outcome. Thus the panel failing to change judgment does not distinguish 'mere compliance' from 'genuine reliability limited by checklist scope.' SRC_002's own countercondition admits this (perturbing decision-relevant features changes the expert but not the panel), yet its proposed test would label that failure as mere compliance, when it could instead reflect a real but incomplete checklist. The evidence criterion is ambiguous between checklist-lossiness and compliance, so it does not reliably distinguish the two as required.","counterexample":[{"SRC_002_test_result":"labels panel as mere compliance, but the panel may still be genuinely reliable on checklist-covered features","expert_judgment":"changes because expert uses richer cues","panel_judgment":"unchanged because checklist does not encode the cue","perturbation":"introduce a subtle cue that is outcome-relevant but not on the checklist"}],"target":"88084a8578c2c06f7a35c5a6d884474485c0470c43ff569f326a4169c578d4f8"}]}